00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:06.990
How did we get here?

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.680
How did this talk happen?

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.070
How did these ads happen?

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.400
Back in December of
2017, Google Cloud

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.570
became the official Cloud
provider for the NCAA.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:24.440
The NCAA has a lot
of typical challenges

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.170
or has a lot of challenges
of a typical enterprise.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:30.200
Although, people may not think
of the NCAA as an enterprise.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.360
But the NCAA manages hundreds
and hundreds of schools

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.800
times the number of sports--

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:37.880
thousands of sports.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.440
It's just not about basketball.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.270
You have football,
baseball, soccer, et cetera.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.630
They have decades and decades
of competition information

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:52.360
and student athlete information.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:57.030
This partnership was grounded
in helping the NCAA build

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.670
the future of their
analytics platform

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.222
to help them understand in more
detail, potentially, what's

00:04:03.222 --> 00:04:04.680
happening with some
of their sports

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.540
and some of the
competitions in areas

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.210
that they may have
had a blind spot to

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.190
or they wanted to get
more insight in to.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.460
The campaign was, Know
What Your Data Knows.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.810
So they have lots of
data, but there were areas

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:18.279
that we wanted to dig deeper.

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:21.399
One of those was supporting
the development of a refined

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:23.360
championship selection process.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.180
So looking at, how
are teams selected

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:30.000
to be in some of the
championships that they manage?

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.530
So the partnership started
in December, kind of based

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:34.835
on these three ideas.

00:04:37.905 --> 00:04:39.655
How many of you are
fans of March Madness?

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:46.220
Now for some of you that
didn't understand or know

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.660
who the NCAA was, March Madness
is a championship tournament

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:52.340
that happens every year.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.540
Guess what month it starts in?

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.620
March.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:02.000
There are 351 NCAA
Division I schools

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.430
that compete for a
spot to participate

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.790
and this 64-team tournament.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.350
There's a women's tournament
and a men's tournament.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:13.350
This talk is primarily focused
on the men's tournament.

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.470
So you have 351 teams.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:21.240
They all are vying to get into
a spot into this tournament.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.860
The NCAA oversees the
selection process--

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:25.260
so which teams get in--

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.120
and then, how they're
seeded into the tournament.

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.190
That process is
very data driven.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.590
Primarily, using
historical game data

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.020
to make decisions
about what teams

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.700
deserve the right to
play in the tournament.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:45.330
Google Cloud is part
of this fantastic

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.370
madness around March Madness.

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.040
We decided that we were going
to build six live television

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.820
ads to run during
the Final Four games.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:59.090
Anyone want to guess how many
games are in the Final Four?

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.700
Three.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.290
So we ran two ads--

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.000
one for each game.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.880
And this is the journey
that we took to build out

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.050
these live television ads.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:12.950
Let's take a trip.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:17.140
How many have you been to
Austin, Texas-- not Austin.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:18.974
How many of you have
been to the Alamodome?

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:30.710
April 2, 2018, there was a
truck sitting right about here.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:33.160
And the National
Championship Game

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.830
was going on inside
of this building.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.830
This is me, right there.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.920
I'm only self-identifiable
by my gray hair.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.470
So we're watching the game and
as soon as the clock hit zero,

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:55.640
zero at halftime,
we built an ad that

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.260
didn't exist prior to the clock
going to 0, 0 from scratch

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.780
and we ran it live on
air, predicting what would

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.330
happen in the second half.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.510
So immerse yourself in
being in this truck.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.610
This truck is roughly, 10 by 12.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:11.540
It's super hot.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:15.150
It's filled with about 13 people
watching a game that about say,

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.650
80-100 million
people are watching--

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.060
I don't know how many
people watch the game,

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:18.880
a lot of people--

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.854
and we have to go run an ad.

00:07:21.854 --> 00:07:23.270
By the way, this
guy right here is

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:27.610
was one of my main
partners on this project

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:29.310
and he doesn't look
too happy right now.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:33.660
Let's watch an ad.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.298
What did we build?

00:07:36.298 --> 00:07:37.292
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:07:37.292 --> 00:07:39.777
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:08.690
[END PLAYBACK]

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:12.500
So that ad ran,
roughly four minutes

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.455
into the beginning of the game.

00:08:14.455 --> 00:08:16.830
Turns out, possessions are
pretty important in basketball

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:20.000
because you can't score if
you don't have the ball.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.160
Turns out, pace is super
important in basketball

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.460
because if you're behind,
you need the ball in order

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:26.100
to score.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.760
So 130 possessions is that good?

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:29.440
Is that bad?

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:30.740
Is that a lot?

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.919
We'll find out, but
that was our estimate,

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:36.750
that was our prediction.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:40.100
So how did we go from
building that ad,

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.780
running that ad,
sitting in that truck--

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:44.330
how did we get there?

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:47.730
This is the overall architecture
that we ended up building.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.720
So we started building this
late November, early December

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.460
right around the start of this
relationship with the NCAA.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.850
Starting on the left hand
side, we have raw data.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:01.970
And then finishing on the right
side, we have a television ad.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.050
Voila, we're done.

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:04.430
We're out.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:08.060
No, there was several pieces
that we had to put together

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:11.720
and we're going to walk
through this left to right.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:15.480
Now if you're not
into product logos

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.980
and you think more in terms
of workflow and tasks,

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:21.970
this is a different way
to look at this talk.

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:24.390
So we're going to talk about
orchestration and subject

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:27.000
matter expertise, business
requirements, et cetera.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.740
You're going to see several--

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.470
probably, 15-- of
these green slides.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:37.550
These are best practices.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:39.216
If you take nothing
away from this talk,

00:09:39.216 --> 00:09:42.710
try to remember what's
on these green slides.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:44.990
I showed you the architecture.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:46.520
Step 1.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.590
If you're going to go
build something like this,

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:52.820
form a team of discrete
subject matter experts.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.000
People who know what they
know really, really well.

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.620
And also, know what
they don't know.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.660
Our team effort
looked like this.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.730
We had basically,
two camps of folks--

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.310
I'll call them,
the techie people

00:10:09.310 --> 00:10:11.740
and the non-techie people.

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:16.000
So the left column, we had a
tech partner lead-- someone

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.560
who worked closely with the NCAA
and other technology groups.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:25.030
We had a gentleman on the
team who is a deep, deep NCAA

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:26.500
basketball expert.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:29.474
We had folks that just
did data engineering.

00:10:29.474 --> 00:10:31.390
Some folks that just did
software engineering.

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.310
Some people were quants.

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:36.580
Other people were just focused
on data science and machine

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.720
learning engineering.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:42.120
Then on the business side, we
had a business partner lead--

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:44.300
again, folks who were working
with the NCAA, working

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:45.680
with Turner, et cetera.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:46.760
People writing copy.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:50.200
Making sure that what we
were doing was on brand.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:52.970
That the ad process
was working right.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.400
PR folks, legal folks.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:57.310
And ultimately, some
editors and one of them

00:10:57.310 --> 00:10:59.400
is in the audience today.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:03.050
So you have this collection
of people and the reason

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:05.810
why I point this out is I've
worked on probably, a hundred

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:06.680
of these projects.

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.500
And I always ask, who's
your data engineer?

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:12.950
And the data science guy or
girl says, we don't have one.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:15.950
That's kind of me, I just
I just do that on the side.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.480
How much time are
you then spending

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.880
doing data engineering versus
actually doing data science

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:20.510
work?

00:11:20.510 --> 00:11:23.030
And you start getting
into this challenge of not

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.980
being able to
focus on the things

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.490
that you're really,
really good at.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.380
Now that's not to say
that it's OK to crossover,

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.459
but if you're trying to
do something really big,

00:11:32.459 --> 00:11:34.250
make sure that you're
staffed appropriately

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.700
around the disciplines that you
need in order to be successful.

00:11:37.700 --> 00:11:39.170
And as you'll see,
there's no way

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.630
that a single machine
learning engineer sitting

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:44.270
in one of our data
centers by themselves

00:11:44.270 --> 00:11:46.742
could pull something like
this off without a ton

00:11:46.742 --> 00:11:47.450
of other support.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:55.520
Next one-- and I promise you,
you'll see some code here soon.

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:58.130
The next one is write down
business requirements.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.720
Scope as soon as possible.

00:11:59.720 --> 00:12:01.520
And communicate with precision.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:04.040
So that group of
technologists talking

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:06.681
to a group of business folks--

00:12:06.681 --> 00:12:08.180
in the beginning,
it was hard for us

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:10.439
to communicate because we
weren't writing down things.

00:12:10.439 --> 00:12:12.480
We're like, yeah, we're
going to build these ads.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:13.970
What do you mean we're
going to build ads?

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.320
How many do we need to build?

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.190
When do they run?

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:17.970
How fast do they
need to get done?

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.860
Et cetera.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:21.950
And then, iteration is key.

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.700
So decisions that we make today
or a decision that we say,

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:28.686
made today, three days
from now more than likely

00:12:28.686 --> 00:12:30.560
we're going to tweak
that based on new inputs

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:34.920
both from the technology side,
as well as the business side.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.784
So how do we get to
business requirements?

00:12:37.784 --> 00:12:39.200
These were words
that I distinctly

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:42.140
remember in meetings.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:44.810
You can guess which ones maybe
come from the business side

00:12:44.810 --> 00:12:46.640
versus the technology side.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.340
So people say hey, we want
these ads to be delightful.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.410
Like, cool.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:53.090
What does that mean?

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.040
And then the folks in
the technology side

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:57.676
is like, how precise
do we have to be?

00:12:57.676 --> 00:13:00.050
And then the legal guys will
be like, don't be too right,

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:01.040
but don't be too wrong.

00:13:06.495 --> 00:13:08.870
As one of the leads on this
project, I was always saying,

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:11.000
is this even feasible?

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:14.480
Can we pull this off?

00:13:14.480 --> 00:13:16.730
So we wrote a bunch
of documents and we

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:20.030
started to get very crisp
and clear about what

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:23.050
a lot of these words meant.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:27.650
From there, we started to
narrow down scope as a means

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.030
to start building a
predictive workflow.

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:31.280
So we had some team rules.

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:33.271
So first and foremost,
this has to be on brand.

00:13:33.271 --> 00:13:34.520
So it had to feel like Google.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.580
It also had to
feel like the NCAA.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:37.580
It had to be contextual.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:38.830
It had to be about basketball.

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:41.270
It had to be about the
game that was happening.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:43.160
Had to have mass appeal.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.770
I wanted to run ads that
were just around dunks,

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.820
but turns out, not
everyone likes dunks.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:52.230
And then we had this idea
of Goldilocks outcome

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:56.990
so it couldn't be too hot and
it also couldn't be too cold.

00:13:56.990 --> 00:13:58.640
Then we had a bunch
of other things,

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.070
like don't be to basketball-y.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:03.620
Needs to feel delightful.

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:05.210
And then-- by the way--

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:08.210
all this system that we were
building on the workflow side

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:10.890
needed to be executed
in under two minutes.

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:14.516
So everything else aside,
soon as that clock hits zero,

00:14:14.516 --> 00:14:15.890
we have two minutes
to basically,

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:18.800
hand off information
to our creative team

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:19.570
to go build an ad.

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:26.026
Then, we finally got to this.

00:14:26.026 --> 00:14:27.650
Wrote this down,
printed it on my wall,

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:29.483
and then didn't sleep
for four months, which

00:14:29.483 --> 00:14:32.180
was build X number of
real-time predictions to power

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.014
the development of
six television ads.

00:14:34.014 --> 00:14:35.930
Each of these have to
run in under 10 minutes,

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.780
from start to finish for
them to getting traffic.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:41.570
With Goldilocks accuracy,
while being prepared

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:43.574
for hundreds of game scenarios.

00:14:43.574 --> 00:14:44.990
How many of you
knew who was going

00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:47.210
to play in the Final Four
games or Championship games

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:50.545
whenever the tournament started?

00:14:50.545 --> 00:14:52.740
Cool.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:56.260
Lot of honest people
in this audience.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.530
So how did we get there?

00:14:58.530 --> 00:15:00.300
Step 1, collect data.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:06.450
Step 3, adds-- that's a
joke from "South Park,"

00:15:06.450 --> 00:15:10.420
probably too old

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.820
So we're going to
work left the right.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:15.730
As we were building
out this new analytics

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:18.840
workflow for the NCAA,
we got very close,

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:25.470
intimate with the
data that they had.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.450
And we're talking about
decades and decades

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.010
worth of competition data.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:33.090
At the same time, we also
worked with a data provider

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.380
called Sport Radar.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:38.760
They are a provider of
real-time sport information

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:43.464
and they also support the NCAA,
as well as Turner Broadcasting.

00:15:43.464 --> 00:15:44.880
And the reason we
worked with them

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.280
is they have real-time
information about what's

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:50.940
happening on the court versus
the majority of the NCAA data

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:54.460
is all historical in nature.

00:15:54.460 --> 00:15:58.110
So step 1, collect data.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:15:59.600
How many of you love XML?

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:06.180
I wrote one of the
first XML parsers

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:08.690
at Microsoft about 15 years ago.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.640
I thought it was the
best thing on the planet.

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:15.560
It turns out that there are
other encoding formats that

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:16.580
are a little bit better.

00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:21.050
But historically, we had to
deal with tens of thousands

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:23.300
of these files that
represented what

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:27.740
happened in these NCAA games.

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:32.600
Some of them are
15,000-20,000 lines long.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.960
How many of you loved JSON?

00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:39.740
Slightly more than
the response from XML.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:43.760
Still, fun format, a little
bit easier to deal with.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.530
The challenge here would be--

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:48.050
in both cases-- what happens
if you have missing data?

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:51.210
What happens if you
have malformed data?

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.040
Tens of thousands of
these files, as well,

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:55.080
for us to just
understand historically

00:16:55.080 --> 00:17:01.620
what happened with all the
games inside of NCAA Division I

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:04.579
basketball.

00:17:04.579 --> 00:17:06.060
So what did we do?

00:17:06.060 --> 00:17:08.089
We went and started
to build a process

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:11.180
to look at the NCAA's data,
as well as Sport Radar data.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:12.443
It just wasn't about games.

00:17:12.443 --> 00:17:13.609
We had to look at schedules.

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:14.810
We had to look at rosters.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.240
We had to look at reference
data, like locations, box

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:18.619
scores, play-by-plays.

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:21.740
Ultimately, we were sitting
on around 100,000 files,

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:23.690
all different types of schema.

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:26.828
By the way, the women's
game has different rules,

00:17:26.828 --> 00:17:28.369
different timing
than the men's game.

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:29.820
So we were like, oh great.

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.910
And then we had to build
two different systems.

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:36.050
We had different primary
keys across this data.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:37.940
Different ways to pull it.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:44.660
So ultimately, we had to build
a workflow around our data

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:46.100
engineering process.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.530
So next best practice--

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:52.100
treat data engineering
as a first-class citizen

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.050
in your overall workflow.

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:56.530
I see this get kicked
to the side a lot,

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:59.180
like, yeah, there's some folks
working on that over there.

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.871
They'll handle all the
ingestion and transformation.

00:18:02.871 --> 00:18:05.120
And also, ingestion is a
function of data engineering,

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.850
so just even acquiring
the data can be

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:09.230
an extremely difficult problem.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:15.170
Next one-- and this is what
you'll see what we built--

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:17.420
is as you start building
out your ingestion

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:19.850
and transformation workflow,
build something that

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:22.550
is repeatable and automatable.

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:26.240
Just because you can go
download a couple of files using

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:31.280
some HTTP-JSON hacked
together implementation,

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:33.920
doesn't mean you can do this
repeatedly over and over

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.280
and over again, potentially
thousands of times at scale.

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:47.046
We used Apache Beam--

00:18:47.046 --> 00:18:48.670
and then, you'll see
here in a minute--

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.510
Cloud Dataflow to drive the
majority of our ingestion

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:54.590
and transformation process.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:57.620
This stuff can be a little
boring because most people just

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.330
want to get to predictions.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:01.040
But as you'll see,
this is probably

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:03.290
one of the most critical
sections in building out

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:06.230
your workflow is being
able to ingest and then

00:19:06.230 --> 00:19:09.650
transform that data into
something that's actionable.

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:13.110
So we leveraged Apache Beam.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:19.640
And then, wrote a series of ETL
pipelines that said, go out,

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:26.830
pull X to Y space from the
NCAA for certain schema types.

00:19:26.830 --> 00:19:30.070
Do some transformation
on that JSON and/or XML.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:35.090
And then, ultimately
load it into BigQuery.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:39.050
The third point about
avoid inline mapping--

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:41.480
you'll see this in
some of the code.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:44.250
I've noticed
sometimes people say,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.440
well, I know that
this game ID over

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:48.950
here or this value over
here maps just directly

00:19:48.950 --> 00:19:50.010
to this column over here.

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:51.885
So all I'm going to do
is just read from here

00:19:51.885 --> 00:19:54.090
and write to there.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:57.090
Try to avoid inline mapping
whenever you're doing this

00:19:57.090 --> 00:20:00.150
because ultimately, something's
going to change in your system

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.760
either on the database
side or on the input side.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.250
So come up with some type
of common type system

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:06.570
that you map to.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.265
And Apache Beam provides an
excellent environment for you

00:20:09.265 --> 00:20:10.890
to create a type
system, whether you're

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:14.110
in Java, Python, et cetera.

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:22.240
We were also writing this
data to a Cloud Storage

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:24.110
to Spanner and to
some other syncs

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:27.170
because we needed the
ability to interact

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:29.910
with this data in different
types of environments.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:32.240
So that's another benefit
of us using Apache Beam,

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:35.000
in this case, is we
read from one source,

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:37.190
but then, we're writing
to multiple syncs.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:49.170
So let's go ahead and
ingest all of 2016.

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:51.500
Here's the code-- some
of the code that I wrote,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:52.709
the pipeline code.

00:20:52.709 --> 00:20:54.000
I'll make this a little bigger.

00:20:57.740 --> 00:21:00.380
It's pretty self-explanatory.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:04.160
So in Apache Beam, I'm going
to go write a transform that

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.860
knows how to go read from
a particular source--

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:09.980
in this case, all the
box score summaries.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:11.870
And then I have a
summary parser that

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:16.640
knows how to take that JSON or
XML, convert it to a Java type,

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:19.250
and then I have a writer.

00:21:19.250 --> 00:21:21.590
And in this case,
my summary writer

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:26.700
ends up splitting a single input
into three different tables.

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:28.417
So I have a summary
about the games.

00:21:28.417 --> 00:21:30.500
I have a summary about the
players in those games.

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:35.200
And I have a summary about
the scoring for those games.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:38.200
And the beauty of this is, is
I can kind of rip and replace

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:39.002
one of my outputs.

00:21:39.002 --> 00:21:40.210
I can push it someplace else.

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:43.510
I can add another
output if I need it.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:48.770
And after I stitch all of
my transforms together,

00:21:48.770 --> 00:21:50.160
I go ahead and hit Run.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:52.500
I could run this locally, but
I would blow up my machine

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:55.020
because we have tens of
thousands of files here.

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:57.790
But I could do that, test
on a few, but let's go ahead

00:21:57.790 --> 00:21:59.400
and run this in the Cloud.

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.370
So hit Run.

00:22:01.370 --> 00:22:04.920
And the command line is going
to package up all my Apache Beam

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:09.260
code, call out to Cloud
Dataflow, kick off a job

00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:10.940
and start reading all my files.

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:18.470
For the sake of
time, we can go look

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:23.300
at what this looks
like in the Console.

00:22:23.300 --> 00:22:28.820
So if I go ahead
and look at this,

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:32.850
here is a view of
one of those jobs.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:36.320
So I have the first
step was basically,

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:37.370
loading my summaries.

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:38.380
Then I have my parser.

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:39.830
Then I have my writer.

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.290
We ran these jobs
thousands and thousands

00:22:42.290 --> 00:22:44.390
and thousands of times.

00:22:44.390 --> 00:22:47.030
And that's the beauty of
building A, a type system

00:22:47.030 --> 00:22:49.970
and then leveraging something
like Apache Beam or another ETL

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:51.980
framework in order to
get this work done.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:02.650
Great.

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:09.940
So in just 2016, a lot of our
focus was around the 15 years

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:12.310
prior to this recent
March Madness.

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:15.100
We were constantly going
back and pulling in 15 years,

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:18.290
15 years, 15 years.

00:23:18.290 --> 00:23:19.850
Now that we have
some data, let's

00:23:19.850 --> 00:23:24.130
start doing some
exploratory analysis.

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:26.200
Next best practice.

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.990
This one I stuck to pretty hard.

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:31.032
If the data wasn't in
BigQuery, it didn't happen.

00:23:31.032 --> 00:23:33.406
We had lots of conversations
with folks on the team like,

00:23:33.406 --> 00:23:35.990
I found this super data or this
feature and it's really great.

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:37.072
And I'm like, where is it?

00:23:37.072 --> 00:23:39.170
Well, it's on my laptop,
or it's in this CSV file,

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:40.180
or I have it in a spreadsheet.

00:23:40.180 --> 00:23:41.030
It's over on GCS.

00:23:41.030 --> 00:23:42.930
I'm like, I don't care.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:46.030
If it's not in BigQuery,
it didn't happen.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:50.030
So consequently, folks
were looking at, well,

00:23:50.030 --> 00:23:51.640
how do I get data into BigQuery?

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:55.160
I don't want to go write
a big Apache Beam job.

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.150
Fortunately, BigQuery has a
nice UI that I can just go

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:01.736
and say go ahead and import
CSV, import Avro, et cetera,

00:24:01.736 --> 00:24:02.860
and get data into BigQuery.

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:12.470
Let's look at some data in
BigQuery that we loaded up.

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:16.020
This is probably, the
simplest run to look at.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:20.040
This is looking at the
total count of games

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.910
by year for men's
Division I basketball.

00:24:23.910 --> 00:24:27.120
I ran this one a lot just as a
verification, like smoke test.

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:31.482
Like yeah, there's roughly,
5,791 games in a year.

00:24:31.482 --> 00:24:33.690
Doesn't really tell you
much, but that's a good audit

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:34.860
process.

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:42.030
And I had all types of different
data outputs from my Apache

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:47.365
Beam jobs or those one-off
jobs in our BigQuery project.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:01.010
The query that I
just showed you is--

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:04.040
I don't know-- not very helpful.

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:08.120
So I'm going to show you another
query which was something

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:10.810
that, as a team,
we quickly realized

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:15.080
was a very valuable thing to
do, which is as we found things

00:25:15.080 --> 00:25:17.270
that were interesting,
we rented them as views

00:25:17.270 --> 00:25:19.230
and then started sharing them.

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:21.050
So instead of just
having some static SQL

00:25:21.050 --> 00:25:22.470
sitting around in source
control somewhere,

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:23.630
it was like, hey, I have a view.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:25.130
It has something
that's interesting.

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:26.570
I'll go ahead and share it.

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:29.060
And a lot of these
views, their goal

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:31.220
were to do things
like grouping by team,

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:32.420
grouping by conference.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:34.340
Doing time series quantization.

00:25:34.340 --> 00:25:35.930
Things that we
had to do in order

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:40.500
to basically, aggregate data
into something meaningful.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:46.200
So if I go here, this query--

00:25:46.200 --> 00:25:47.950
this is one of the
original ones we wrote.

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:49.750
We were interested
to see, is there

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:53.530
a difference amongst
conferences-- there's

00:25:53.530 --> 00:25:55.720
32 conferences in
Division I basketball--

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.720
is there a difference among
conferences in their home court

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:00.560
advantage?

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:03.500
Do you think that there's a
difference among conferences

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:06.990
in home court advantage?

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:09.930
Maybe, I don't know.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.720
So I write this query.

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:14.890
It's interesting, it
shows me some data,

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:19.402
but it's kind of hard to
see what this really means.

00:26:19.402 --> 00:26:21.360
So you kind of have a
hunch that, yeah, there's

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:24.960
probably a difference
in home court advantage.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:27.330
One of the nice features
inside of BigQuery

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:31.680
is I can click on the
outputs of my query

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:35.040
and say, visualize
inside of Data Studio.

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:40.350
And in this case, I want
to look at the home point

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:44.260
advantage, which was
the output of my query.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:53.800
And I can quickly
visualize that yes, there

00:26:53.800 --> 00:26:59.020
is a difference between
PAC-12 playing at home.

00:26:59.020 --> 00:27:02.350
They're favored by about four
and a half points on average

00:27:02.350 --> 00:27:07.276
versus the Ivy League
or the Big South or MAC.

00:27:07.276 --> 00:27:08.900
So I haven't built
any predictions yet,

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:11.260
but I'm starting to kind of
gain some insight on what

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:12.910
things could be
skillful in terms

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:14.790
of building out predictions.

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:18.130
Say if you have a PAC-12
team playing a Colonial

00:27:18.130 --> 00:27:22.320
team during March Madness.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.790
Keep in mind,
though, that they're

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:26.470
playing on neutral courts so we
have another problem to solve,

00:27:26.470 --> 00:27:29.670
but this is an amazing way
to start exploring data.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:35.440
By the way, Data
Studio's free and all

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.830
of the visualizations that
you build inside of it

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:39.690
are easily shareable.

00:27:39.690 --> 00:27:42.967
If you've played with Google
Docs, go ahead and click Share,

00:27:42.967 --> 00:27:44.300
you'll have access to your data.

00:27:50.074 --> 00:27:50.990
Don't want to do that.

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:00.150
So we can explore
data in BigQuery.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:04.200
We built views to
satisfy common use cases.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:06.332
We also spent some time
looking at our audit logs

00:28:06.332 --> 00:28:08.790
and just talking to folks, like
hey, what are you building?

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:09.290
What are you writing?

00:28:09.290 --> 00:28:11.041
And they're like, I
wrote this cool query.

00:28:11.041 --> 00:28:12.790
Great, let's go ahead
and materialize that

00:28:12.790 --> 00:28:15.251
as a viewer as a table so you
stop running the query over

00:28:15.251 --> 00:28:17.542
and over again and you get
a little bit more efficient.

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:23.370
The next thing that we did
was look at some visualization

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:26.160
techniques outside of
BigQuery and Data Studio,

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:34.900
which is using Cloud Datalab.

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:40.270
So Cloud Datalab is a
hostable implementation

00:28:40.270 --> 00:28:41.780
of IPython Notebook.

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:44.020
You can run it locally
in a docker container.

00:28:44.020 --> 00:28:47.360
You could also run
it on the Cloud.

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:50.110
Go ahead and just use
our gcloud commands

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:52.360
to go create a Datalab instance.

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:56.260
And I have a fully
fleshed out IPython

00:28:56.260 --> 00:29:00.910
development environment that is
optimized for the Google Cloud.

00:29:00.910 --> 00:29:08.620
And once you're there, you
can do something like this.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:11.720
I'm in my IPython Notebook--

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:14.690
make this a little bigger--

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.660
and I take my BigQuery
that I wrote--

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:20.030
this is for a different
type of analysis--

00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:22.790
move it over into Datalab.

00:29:22.790 --> 00:29:25.590
I execute that query.

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:27.900
It's a fairly big query.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:32.670
I pump it into a data frame
and do some visualization.

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:34.890
So this visualization
was focused

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:38.730
on understanding how players
on teams on an individual basis

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:43.590
can contribute to the three
points that a team is scoring.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:46.020
So in this case,
Kentucky on average

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:48.000
has three players
that contribute

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.750
the majority of their
three-point production

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:55.230
versus Davidson, who has
five different folks who

00:29:55.230 --> 00:29:58.272
are contributing to the
three-point production.

00:29:58.272 --> 00:29:59.730
So you could look
at this and start

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:03.390
to make some inferences, like
three guys versus five guys who

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:05.430
are able to produce
three-pointers,

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:06.854
who has the advantage?

00:30:16.740 --> 00:30:18.370
So I can visualize
in Data Studio,

00:30:18.370 --> 00:30:19.810
sitting on top of BigQuery.

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:22.300
I can visualize
inside of Datalab

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:25.280
sitting on top of a BigQuery.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.860
By the way, if you go out
to medium.com and look

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:30.880
for analyzing NCAA college
basketball with GCP,

00:30:30.880 --> 00:30:35.430
we have about 30 articles
that reinforces some of the--

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:38.540
we'll call them,
classic visualizations

00:30:38.540 --> 00:30:41.689
and feature ideas that
we were coming up with.

00:30:41.689 --> 00:30:43.730
So the best practice here,
if you think something

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:45.200
is interesting, visualize it.

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:48.110
If you don't know if something
is interesting, visualize it.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:50.720
If you find something that
you thought was interesting

00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:53.105
and it turns out not to be
interesting, visualize it

00:30:53.105 --> 00:30:54.230
and share it with the team.

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.469
So now we're going to go
from exploratory analysis

00:31:01.469 --> 00:31:02.635
to looking at some features.

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:09.580
Some things to keep in mind,
all 351 teams in Division I

00:31:09.580 --> 00:31:11.887
basketball are not equal in
strength and performance.

00:31:11.887 --> 00:31:14.470
If that was the case, man, this
would be a really, really hard

00:31:14.470 --> 00:31:17.424
tournament to pick the teams.

00:31:17.424 --> 00:31:19.090
But in our case, we
wouldn't necessarily

00:31:19.090 --> 00:31:21.779
know who all the Final Four
teams were until a week out,

00:31:21.779 --> 00:31:23.320
so as we were thinking
about features

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:25.330
and what type of
models we would build,

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:28.690
we had a plan for a very,
very broad set of use cases.

00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:36.409
From there, as we started
to drive into the features

00:31:36.409 --> 00:31:38.700
that we were going to use,
then we had to stop and say,

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:39.790
what are we predicting on?

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:41.040
We still haven't gotten there.

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:43.620
So the conversations between
the tech folks and the business

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:46.155
folks, we came down to
these 28 predictions.

00:31:46.155 --> 00:31:48.030
So we had predictions
focused on single team.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:50.370
And we had predictions
focused on combined stats

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:51.620
between teams.

00:31:51.620 --> 00:31:55.215
And then we also had these
predictions around game state.

00:31:55.215 --> 00:31:59.830
We focused on close and
late and down to the wire.

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.410
In essence, we had to go
build 28 predictions for each

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:04.746
of these games across
these categories.

00:32:08.560 --> 00:32:14.150
So this is the number
of predictions.

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:17.900
And then looking into those
prediction categories,

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:20.250
we started looking at
what's interesting?

00:32:20.250 --> 00:32:23.420
What makes three-point
attempts interesting?

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:25.094
Or possessions interesting?

00:32:25.094 --> 00:32:26.510
So before you saw
up there, it was

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:31.280
said 130 was the estimate for
the number of possessions.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:34.460
So on this slide, we
looked at the distribution

00:32:34.460 --> 00:32:39.410
of all of these stats
across 2017 men's Division I

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:39.980
basketball.

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:43.700
And it turns out,
130 possessions

00:32:43.700 --> 00:32:49.580
would be roughly, around
the 50th percentile--

00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:51.690
down in the bottom.

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:54.249
Now keep in mind, this
is across all teams.

00:32:54.249 --> 00:32:55.790
We don't necessarily
know who's going

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:58.792
to play in the Championship
Game or Final Four,

00:32:58.792 --> 00:33:00.500
but this gives you
some indication about,

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:01.460
what is interesting?

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:03.467
Where would those
numbers fall if you

00:33:03.467 --> 00:33:04.550
were looking at all teams?

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:09.570
So the next thing
to do was we knew

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:12.330
which prediction categories
we wanted to go after.

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.190
We knew potentially,
what was interesting.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:17.589
Then we had to figure out, what
type of modeling techniques

00:33:17.589 --> 00:33:18.630
are we going to use here?

00:33:18.630 --> 00:33:19.980
Are we going to use
regression techniques?

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:22.090
Are we going to use
classification techniques?

00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:25.170
So if you're new to this,
this is a real quick slide.

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:26.760
The idea of regression
is I'm going

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:31.170
to take some input
variables and then, output

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:32.634
some continuous variable.

00:33:32.634 --> 00:33:34.800
And the whole idea is I'm
trying to build a function

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:36.210
to do this--

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:37.840
the magic of machine learning.

00:33:37.840 --> 00:33:40.410
So my input variables would
be all these different stats

00:33:40.410 --> 00:33:43.140
and the output variable
would be how many possessions

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:44.520
are going to be in this game.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:46.978
How many three-point attempts
are going to be in this game.

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:51.280
From a classification
perspective,

00:33:51.280 --> 00:33:53.890
again, n number of
variables and then,

00:33:53.890 --> 00:33:56.140
output some discrete variable.

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:58.960
So is this game going
to be close and late?

00:33:58.960 --> 00:33:59.980
Yes or no?

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:04.210
So you have some type of
binary classification.

00:34:04.210 --> 00:34:06.020
The question here is, what is X?

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.500
What features should you
use to drive these models?

00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:13.030
This is the hardest part.

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:15.639
This is like saying--

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:18.239
I had some funny things to say--

00:34:18.239 --> 00:34:20.526
it's like, where the
babies come from?

00:34:20.526 --> 00:34:22.150
We kind of know where
babies come from,

00:34:22.150 --> 00:34:25.210
but the process, there's
all different types

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:27.400
of things that happen.

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:29.650
And how do you stitch
all of these things

00:34:29.650 --> 00:34:34.600
together in order to create some
type of successful function?

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:38.530
And this is the whole process of
doing your feature exploration

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:41.080
and feature engineering.

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:44.500
So going back to our data,
we started talking to folks

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:46.840
and say, we have data about
conference, and seasons,

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:49.870
and games, and players,
and segments, et cetera.

00:34:49.870 --> 00:34:52.000
Oh, by the way, we're
not using one tool.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:55.540
We're using Tableau and
Jupiter and RStudio and Colab,

00:34:55.540 --> 00:34:56.350
et cetera.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:35:00.670
But everyone was able to
rally around using BigQuery

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:03.190
as the center of
truth for the data.

00:35:03.190 --> 00:35:04.840
You want to use RStudio?

00:35:04.840 --> 00:35:06.370
Awesome, rock.

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:08.200
Easy connectivity to BigQuery.

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:09.520
You're in Colab?

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:11.970
Go for it.

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:15.210
Everyone was looking
at the same data.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:23.850
From here, we can then look at
what features could potentially

00:35:23.850 --> 00:35:26.010
be skillful.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:28.170
This was a model that we wrote.

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:30.390
It was a classification
feature to see

00:35:30.390 --> 00:35:34.590
if a game would fall into
higher than the 75th percentile

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:36.330
for a particular stat.

00:35:36.330 --> 00:35:38.887
I think in this case, it
was three-point attempts.

00:35:38.887 --> 00:35:41.220
So we would start with hundreds
and hundreds of features

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.050
and using different
techniques, we

00:35:43.050 --> 00:35:46.560
could start to look at which
features were most skillful.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:51.355
Which ones were going to give
signal to drive a prediction.

00:35:51.355 --> 00:35:52.980
Interestingly enough,
if you're looking

00:35:52.980 --> 00:35:56.730
at three-point attempts, one
of the most skillful features

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:59.390
is the average of three-point
attempts by a team

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:02.470
in their last seven games.

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:06.630
That's an easy one, but if
you only had one feature,

00:36:06.630 --> 00:36:09.040
your model may not
be very powerful.

00:36:09.040 --> 00:36:10.800
So we go searching
for more features

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:13.450
and looking at things like--

00:36:13.450 --> 00:36:15.990
let me scroll down here
and find another one that's

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:17.730
interesting--

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:20.981
free throw attempts in
[? an average ?] seven games.

00:36:20.981 --> 00:36:22.980
You can see there's a
little bit of distribution

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:26.470
between these two.

00:36:26.470 --> 00:36:28.980
So there are
different techniques

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:31.480
to go through your
feature selection process.

00:36:31.480 --> 00:36:34.080
The point here is that whatever
tool that you're choosing

00:36:34.080 --> 00:36:37.270
has access to the same data.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:38.950
And as we were doing our work--

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:42.010
one of the guys in the
audience was on the team--

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:44.580
we would be saying,
in my process,

00:36:44.580 --> 00:36:47.870
I see that this feature
is really skillful.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:49.774
Great, go ahead and
put it back into a view

00:36:49.774 --> 00:36:50.940
so then everyone can use it.

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:00.730
So best practice.

00:37:00.730 --> 00:37:04.200
So use views to help
build out your features

00:37:04.200 --> 00:37:05.640
that you're selecting.

00:37:05.640 --> 00:37:07.290
Version them-- this
was another thing

00:37:07.290 --> 00:37:09.330
that people were
like, a week ago I

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:12.140
had a feature that seemed to
be pretty skillful, but now

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:13.140
where did that thing go?

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:15.060
So use views and
just version them.

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:20.370
Another best practice,
use SQL as much

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:24.860
as possible to build the
aggregations for your features.

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:27.470
I had lots of conversations
with people and they would say,

00:37:27.470 --> 00:37:32.345
I wrote this wicked pandas code
in IPython that extracts data.

00:37:32.345 --> 00:37:34.485
It does a bunch of
aggregation and loops

00:37:34.485 --> 00:37:35.610
and blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:36.776
And I have awesome features.

00:37:36.776 --> 00:37:38.241
I'm like, great.

00:37:38.241 --> 00:37:38.990
Could I have that?

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:41.800
And then they send the code,
check it in, and I run it.

00:37:41.800 --> 00:37:45.230
I'm like, hey, this takes
an hour and a half to run.

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:47.840
And 95% of that could
have been done in SQL,

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:50.210
could we take that process
and put it into SQL

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:52.640
so that I can just leverage
BigQuery to push all that down

00:37:52.640 --> 00:37:54.900
and run it in 12 seconds?

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:58.670
Now granted, there are some
cases where you can't do that,

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:00.720
but if you're not
trying to do it,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.630
then you won't
necessarily know at what

00:38:02.630 --> 00:38:06.470
point do you need to jump out
of some hand-crafted environment

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:08.770
and leverage SQL.

00:38:11.257 --> 00:38:13.215
Other best practice--
all engineering artifacts

00:38:13.215 --> 00:38:14.464
should live in source control.

00:38:14.464 --> 00:38:16.590
Persistent disks
attached to VMs does not

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:18.600
qualify as source control.

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:20.430
Your laptop that
you think is secure

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:22.950
does not satisfy
as source control.

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:25.789
Build a system, get whatever
workflow you want to use,

00:38:25.789 --> 00:38:27.330
make sure you're
checking everything.

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:28.496
So it's just not about code.

00:38:28.496 --> 00:38:29.160
It's about SQL.

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:30.449
It's about your notebooks.

00:38:30.449 --> 00:38:31.365
It's about your ideas.

00:38:35.900 --> 00:38:38.320
So we talked about
some feature analysis.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:44.508
Now let's get into modeling
and make some predictions.

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:57.900
So we use five different
tools to build out our models.

00:38:57.900 --> 00:39:00.360
And we use BigQuery ML--

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:02.640
which was announced yesterday--

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:07.470
and it was a lifesaver
on this project.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:09.180
We used Scikit-learn heavily.

00:39:09.180 --> 00:39:10.860
We used XGBoost.

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.710
We used Keras and a
little bit of TensorFlow.

00:39:13.710 --> 00:39:16.660
Again, the whole idea was--
whether we were building

00:39:16.660 --> 00:39:18.840
a regression model or a
classification model--

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:22.080
we were using these
tools because these

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:25.920
are the tools that were helping
us build the best performance.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:30.980
Not necessarily because it
was the tool that we liked.

00:39:30.980 --> 00:39:33.230
So you'll see this in a minute--

00:39:33.230 --> 00:39:35.960
choose the tool that works, not
the tool that you like to use.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:46.820
And I'm going to jump over to--

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:49.100
the first one that
I'm going to show you

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:51.044
is our close and late model.

00:39:51.044 --> 00:39:52.460
How many of you
would like to know

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:56.090
if a game was going to end close
and late at halftime so you

00:39:56.090 --> 00:40:01.370
knew whether you should
just leave or stay at home?

00:40:01.370 --> 00:40:03.800
So we built this
close and late model

00:40:03.800 --> 00:40:07.691
and the person that built
it did all the right things.

00:40:07.691 --> 00:40:09.440
They wrote down the
business requirements.

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:11.810
They were very, very
precise about what

00:40:11.810 --> 00:40:13.280
this definition
of close and late

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:16.300
meant versus down
to the wire meant.

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:19.580
There's all the SQL that were
driving the feature inputs.

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:23.950
And talked about what types
of features that matter.

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:27.230
And then, ultimately, we
built some visualization

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:30.180
to show if this model
would trigger or not.

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:33.170
So our minimum threshold
for running an ad

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:35.960
around a prediction
was at minimum,

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:37.970
it had to be 65% confident.

00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:41.450
So 65% probability that
this was going to happen.

00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:42.950
Otherwise, we
wouldn't move forward.

00:40:42.950 --> 00:40:47.090
So at halftime, the Villanova
score was 37, Michigan 28.

00:40:47.090 --> 00:40:49.430
So Villanova was up by nine.

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:53.660
And if you look at this
graphic, Villanova's down here.

00:40:53.660 --> 00:40:56.750
Here's Villanova whenever
they're up by 10.

00:40:56.750 --> 00:41:02.840
We weren't even close to hitting
65% probability on this, which

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:08.390
was awesome because we knew that
this was a decision we were not

00:41:08.390 --> 00:41:10.310
going to make.

00:41:10.310 --> 00:41:12.785
We would not run this ad.

00:41:12.785 --> 00:41:13.910
So this was close and late.

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:17.540
And the sad part is that
none of the Final Four games

00:41:17.540 --> 00:41:19.640
ended up close and late.

00:41:19.640 --> 00:41:24.865
And we did a ton of work
and never ran the ad.

00:41:24.865 --> 00:41:26.206
D'oh!

00:41:26.206 --> 00:41:27.320
But it was a lot of fun.

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:34.049
Another modeling technique
that we used was BigQuery ML.

00:41:34.049 --> 00:41:35.590
So now we're back
into BigQuery, so I

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:37.090
took all those steps
of like feature

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:39.460
engineering, visualization,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:41.440
Now with BigQuery ML,
I have the ability

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:45.370
to build a model straight
in line and here's the code.

00:41:45.370 --> 00:41:47.800
Basically, you say
create a replace model,

00:41:47.800 --> 00:41:51.950
which is functionally just a
view inside of your data set.

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:53.590
I go ahead and
select the features

00:41:53.590 --> 00:41:55.750
that I want to use so I
go ahead and selection

00:41:55.750 --> 00:41:59.770
this really, really, really
wide view of features.

00:41:59.770 --> 00:42:02.290
Go ahead [? and train. ?]

00:42:02.290 --> 00:42:04.930
In this case, I wanted to
build a linear regression model

00:42:04.930 --> 00:42:08.810
and it will output a model
for me then, to score.

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:12.070
So I've already
built this model.

00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:14.140
I'll go ahead and flip
to the other view, which

00:42:14.140 --> 00:42:16.170
is now I want to predict.

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:21.040
And I say, I need to
select from the game

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:22.310
that I want to look at.

00:42:22.310 --> 00:42:25.786
So in this case, it was
the Championship Game.

00:42:25.786 --> 00:42:27.910
I'm going to go ahead and
pull in all the stats, up

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:33.661
through the first half
and then run the query.

00:42:33.661 --> 00:42:34.160
Check that.

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:36.980
This was all the stats
prior right up to the game.

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:39.890
So we were trying to predict
on total three-point attempts.

00:42:39.890 --> 00:42:42.890
So our estimate for
that game was 50%--

00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:48.210
sorry, the estimate was 49.85%
and the actual value was 50%.

00:42:48.210 --> 00:42:52.130
So we had 28 different versions
of these models predicting

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:55.010
on all different stats
and we would run them

00:42:55.010 --> 00:42:57.920
in real-time for us
to then, build out

00:42:57.920 --> 00:43:01.400
a decision tree for which
ad we would run on TV.

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:12.070
So we've gotten all the
way through this process

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:15.010
and we're sitting in
the truck and we're

00:43:15.010 --> 00:43:18.190
ready to make a decision.

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:20.740
How did we do it?

00:43:20.740 --> 00:43:24.640
By the way, I can't underscore
the amount of pressure

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:30.130
that we were feeling because
we had to deliver an ad.

00:43:30.130 --> 00:43:34.820
And that's kind of fun.

00:43:34.820 --> 00:43:35.960
So what did we do?

00:43:35.960 --> 00:43:39.310
We had automation for
serving each path.

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:42.550
So we had automation for
people using BigQuery.

00:43:42.550 --> 00:43:44.930
We had automation for people
that were using TensorFlow.

00:43:44.930 --> 00:43:47.320
And in some cases, some of
our automation was manual.

00:43:47.320 --> 00:43:49.520
We had a person whose
job it was, hey,

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:50.500
I'm running this model.

00:43:50.500 --> 00:43:51.666
This is how the thing works.

00:43:51.666 --> 00:43:53.450
Here's the data
inputs that I need.

00:43:53.450 --> 00:43:55.960
But the outputs of
all the automation

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:58.360
drove everything
into Google Sheets.

00:43:58.360 --> 00:44:01.720
It's a lo-fi way, low-tech
to do it and it turns out,

00:44:01.720 --> 00:44:04.300
it worked really, really well
because we could easily share

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:07.120
all this data with everyone.

00:44:07.120 --> 00:44:09.340
We had to do nothing
to build it out other

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:12.070
than write some script and our
automation that says, right

00:44:12.070 --> 00:44:14.410
now, here's the state
of all the predictions

00:44:14.410 --> 00:44:15.790
that we want to make.

00:44:15.790 --> 00:44:17.860
Pick the one that
feels the best.

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:22.060
And at halftime, this is
what we ended up looking at,

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:26.970
which was we ended up picking
three-points attempt combined

00:44:26.970 --> 00:44:29.050
at the second half.

00:44:29.050 --> 00:44:35.520
And with that data, we then
moved over to another system.

00:44:35.520 --> 00:44:39.340
So part of this process
for this overall workflow,

00:44:39.340 --> 00:44:42.260
are the ad agency and creative
agency that we worked with--

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:45.710
Eleven, here in San Francisco--

00:44:45.710 --> 00:44:48.830
built out an
encoding system that

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:51.170
ran on the Cloud that
could take inputs

00:44:51.170 --> 00:44:56.177
and then encode a live
ad in around 30 seconds.

00:44:56.177 --> 00:44:57.760
So this was the
workflow that we used.

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:00.250
So we took the output
of our predictions

00:45:00.250 --> 00:45:05.920
put them into the
encoding environment

00:45:05.920 --> 00:45:08.770
and then, boom, outputs an ad.

00:45:08.770 --> 00:45:10.360
What I don't show
you here is we had

00:45:10.360 --> 00:45:12.910
a war room of around 100
different scenarios that

00:45:12.910 --> 00:45:14.410
could possibly be happening.

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:16.300
It just wasn't around
three-point attempts.

00:45:16.300 --> 00:45:20.020
It was three-point attempts
and the underdog was winning.

00:45:20.020 --> 00:45:25.300
Or possessions and the
favorite team is up by 50.

00:45:25.300 --> 00:45:26.800
We had all these
different scenarios

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:29.560
that we had to come up with
because the visualization

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:32.680
that you ultimately see
in the ad had to match

00:45:32.680 --> 00:45:34.021
the context of the game.

00:45:34.021 --> 00:45:35.770
Just because I could
give you a prediction

00:45:35.770 --> 00:45:38.020
doesn't necessarily mean
it would resonate well,

00:45:38.020 --> 00:45:40.270
unless the visuals met
the context of the game.

00:45:46.320 --> 00:45:50.750
So with that, then we
had that encoded ad

00:45:50.750 --> 00:45:52.070
and we trafficked it.

00:45:52.070 --> 00:45:54.560
And that was a super fun process
because we were literally

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:58.310
on the phone with the
folks at Turner and CBS--

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:01.310
their ad trafficking people--
and said, yeah, we got your ad.

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:02.150
It's going to run.

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:04.790
And we sat there and
about two minutes later,

00:46:04.790 --> 00:46:07.272
that ad was on
national television.

00:46:07.272 --> 00:46:09.230
Eight minutes prior, nine
minutes prior, the ad

00:46:09.230 --> 00:46:10.580
didn't exist.

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:13.730
The prediction didn't exist.

00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:16.910
I remember, we have this
verification process

00:46:16.910 --> 00:46:19.850
to understand what happened
when and why and I think in one

00:46:19.850 --> 00:46:21.650
of the ads, there was a cat--

00:46:21.650 --> 00:46:24.227
I think-- on the
back of a turtle--

00:46:24.227 --> 00:46:24.810
is that right?

00:46:24.810 --> 00:46:26.601
Yeah, there was a cat
on a back of a turtle

00:46:26.601 --> 00:46:30.140
and you hear our auditor
talking to their auditor.

00:46:30.140 --> 00:46:32.210
And the lady says,
yes, I can confirm

00:46:32.210 --> 00:46:34.810
that there is a cat on
the back of the turtle.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:37.850
And then they hang up the
phone and then the ad runs.

00:46:40.820 --> 00:46:44.160
So another best practice
was we implemented a process

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:47.910
to audit the chain of command
regarding decision making.

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:50.220
So as those predictions
came out of the system,

00:46:50.220 --> 00:46:51.220
we were looking at them.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:54.710
We had a whole process to
figure out, this is good.

00:46:54.710 --> 00:46:55.410
This is bad.

00:46:55.410 --> 00:46:57.830
This reaches threshold,
this doesn't, et cetera.

00:46:57.830 --> 00:46:58.820
Write everything down.

00:46:58.820 --> 00:47:00.500
Who said what, when?

00:47:00.500 --> 00:47:02.920
And then go.

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:04.730
Especially if you
have humans involved,

00:47:04.730 --> 00:47:06.380
this is of paramount importance.

00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:08.160
If this was
completely automated,

00:47:08.160 --> 00:47:10.887
you would still have an
audit log because frequently,

00:47:10.887 --> 00:47:13.220
something's going to happen
with some automated process.

00:47:13.220 --> 00:47:15.380
You'll have bugs and
you need to go fix it.

00:47:15.380 --> 00:47:17.000
So definitely
implement some type

00:47:17.000 --> 00:47:21.570
of audit process for
this type of flow.

00:47:21.570 --> 00:47:23.230
So how did we do?

00:47:23.230 --> 00:47:28.470
We had six different games
and what we ran on TV

00:47:28.470 --> 00:47:31.890
were the implied
probabilities for each one

00:47:31.890 --> 00:47:32.830
of our predictions.

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:34.470
So we had an estimate.

00:47:34.470 --> 00:47:36.030
We had some type
of continuous value

00:47:36.030 --> 00:47:38.640
that came out of our
prediction models

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:40.710
and you can see down
at the bottom, where

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:44.070
we said second half total
three-point attempts combined,

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:45.930
the estimate was 24.

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:47.200
The result was 24.

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:48.330
So we nailed the estimate.

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:52.530
The ad that actually ran
was we will see at least 21

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:57.780
of these things at a
76.4% confidence level.

00:47:57.780 --> 00:47:59.700
And the reason
why we did this is

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:01.770
that a television
ad, we couldn't

00:48:01.770 --> 00:48:04.230
put things like confidence
intervals and plus/minus,

00:48:04.230 --> 00:48:06.510
and stuff in the ads so we
did some work to basically,

00:48:06.510 --> 00:48:09.810
take our estimate and convert
it into a probability.

00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:10.770
So we nailed that one.

00:48:10.770 --> 00:48:14.220
We nailed the combined
possessions one.

00:48:14.220 --> 00:48:17.670
Total shot attempts-- we got
a little squirrelly there

00:48:17.670 --> 00:48:21.210
because the Kansas-Villanova
game kind of got out of hand.

00:48:21.210 --> 00:48:22.650
And that's the
whole fun of this.

00:48:22.650 --> 00:48:23.820
It's like you think
you know what's

00:48:23.820 --> 00:48:26.111
going to happen in the game
and then the game gets away

00:48:26.111 --> 00:48:28.360
and sometimes, your
estimates are off.

00:48:32.120 --> 00:48:34.980
If you want to learn
more about this process,

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.670
we built half of
a basketball court

00:48:37.670 --> 00:48:40.130
in the bottom of Moscone South.

00:48:40.130 --> 00:48:42.440
How many of you have
been down there yet?

00:48:42.440 --> 00:48:44.270
Cool.

00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:46.430
Feel free to come down.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:49.790
I can get into any part
of this that you'd like.

00:48:49.790 --> 00:48:54.040
And we also have a real-time
motion capture system set up

00:48:54.040 --> 00:48:59.420
that you can see how good
or bad your jump shot is.

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:03.410
And it's a fun way to
get into data science

00:49:03.410 --> 00:49:05.810
from a different angle,
just looking at scores like,

00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:06.500
that's fun.

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:09.320
You can build live
TV ads, that's OK.

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:13.850
But seeing yourself in
real-time shooting a jumper,

00:49:13.850 --> 00:49:15.600
that's pretty fun.

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:19.950
So with that said, I'll
stop, take some questions.

00:49:19.950 --> 00:49:23.370
And I hope you have
a great rest of Next.

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:24.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:28.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

